            <ArticleTitle>[Migraine and temporomandibular joint dysfunction: mechanisms of comorbidity].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33-38</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.17116/jnevro201511510133-38</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to analyze the frequency of different forms of temporomandibular joint dysfunction (TMJD) in episodic and chronic migraine and to study mechanisms of their comorbidity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty-three patients with chronic migraine (CM) and 40 patients with episodic migraine (EM) were examined. Anxiety and depression were assessed with the HADS, quality-of-life related to headache was measured with the HIT-6. To evaluate the antinociceptive system function, we studied blink reflex (BR) and nociceptive flexion reflex (NFR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The higher frequency of TMJD was identified in patients with CM compared to those with EM (47.5 and 28.5%, respectively), CM patients more frequently had disturbances related to the myofascial pain syndrome of masticatory muscles. R3 BR and NFR thresholds were significantly decreased in CM patients as well.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">TMJD is a component of the pathogenesis in many patients with CM. Masticatory muscles may generate nociceptive impulses in CNS, ie be a source of peripheral sensitization, that promote pain chronification, including migraine. Dysfunction of masticatory muscles may reflect the central sensitization and disturbance of efferent pain control.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
